               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate when-custom="12XX" notBefore="1200" notAfter="1299"
                        calendar="#gregorian">s. xiii</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance><orgName role="fmo" key="org_488">Scarborough, Yorkshire. Grange or
                        'domus' dependent on the abbey of Cîteaux</orgName><p>'Liber Cistercii'
                        (fols. 1r, 110r, 208v).</p><p>Letters on fols. 77v–78r show that the book
                        was temporarily brought to Scarborough, a cell of Citeaux, by Hugh, monk of
                        Citeaux c. 1295, and that it was a short time at the Cistercian Rewley abbey
                        near Oxford. See A. H. Thompson, <title>Proc. Leeds Phil. Soc.</title> (Lit.
                        and Hist. Section), 1 (1928) 238–258.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">Phillips</persName>
                     3590; sale 17 May 1897 lot 51.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">Dr Caton</persName>,
                     pro-Chancellor of Liverpool University.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Newbury, <persName role="fmo">Prof. H. A.
                        Ormerod</persName>, who married Caton's daughter. This was the location
                     given in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964). The
                     supplement to Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1987) provided the following
                     update: 'Prof. Ormerod is deceased and the MS cannot now be
                     traced'.</provenance>
               </history>
